# Requests for the VS Code REST Client or JetBrains HTTP Client.
# Start the demo first: npm start
# Send them in order and watch the x-cache response headers.

@proxy = http://127.0.0.1:4000/graphql
@origin = http://127.0.0.1:4001/graphql

### 1. All books: MISS the first time, HIT the second time
POST {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ books { id title author } }"
}

### 2. One book by id: change the variable to get a separate cache entry
POST {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "query Book($id: ID!) { book(id: $id) { id title author } }",
  "variables": { "id": "1" }
}

### 3. Authenticated request: always BYPASS (demo token, not a real secret)
POST {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer demo-token-alice

{
  "query": "{ books { id title author } }"
}

### 4. Update book 1 through the cache: BYPASS, invalidates cached entries containing book 1
POST {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation { updateBook(id: \"1\", title: \"Updated Book Title\") { id title } }"
}

### 5. Update book 1 directly at the origin: the cache does not notice (stale HIT afterwards)
POST {{origin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation { updateBook(id: \"1\", title: \"Changed Behind The Cache\") { id title } }"
}

### 6. The same read with the HTTP QUERY method (RFC 10008), shares the entry of request 1
QUERY {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ books { id title author } }"
}

### 7. A mutation over QUERY: rejected with 405
QUERY {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation { updateBook(id: \"1\", title: \"Updated Book Title\") { id title } }"
}

### 8. Introspection: BYPASS
POST {{proxy}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ __schema { queryType { name } } }"
}

### 9. Cache statistics
GET http://127.0.0.1:4000/_cache/stats
